#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# unit_convert

#% text
# `unit_convert(q, unit)` gives the quantity `q` in another unit of the same dimension.

#% text
# `unit_convert(q)` gives `q` in SI base units.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Conversion factors are exact, so exact magnitudes stay exact.

#% item
# Temperatures in kelvins, degrees Celsius and Fahrenheit convert with their offsets.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
unit_convert(quantity(1, "Miles"), "Kilometers")
#% output
#= quantity(25146/15625, "Kilometers")

#% cell
unit_convert(quantity(100, "Kilometers"/"Hours"), "Meters"/"Seconds")
#% output
#= quantity(250/9, "Meters"/"Seconds")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% cell
unit_convert(quantity(30, "DegreesCelsius"), "DegreesFahrenheit")
#% output
#= quantity(86, "DegreesFahrenheit")

#% cell
unit_convert(quantity(1, "Newtons"))
#% output
#= quantity(1, "Kilograms"*"Meters"/"Seconds"^2)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `quantity`, `quantity_magnitude`
